Just as {@link Ext.dom.Element Ext.Element} wraps around a native DOM node, Ext.EventObject
wraps the browser's native event-object normalizing cross-browser differences,
such as which mouse button is clicked, keys pressed, mechanisms to stop
event-propagation along with a method to prevent default actions from taking place.

For example:

    function handleClick(e, t){ // e is not a standard event object, it is a Ext.EventObject
        e.preventDefault();
        var target = e.getTarget(); // same as t (the target HTMLElement)
        ...
    }

    var myDiv = {@link Ext#method!get Ext.get}("myDiv");  // get reference to an {@link Ext.dom.Element Ext.Element}
    myDiv.on(         // 'on' is shorthand for addListener
        "click",      // perform an action on click of myDiv
        handleClick   // reference to the action handler
    );

    // other methods to do the same:
    Ext.EventManager.on("myDiv", 'click', handleClick);
    Ext.EventManager.addListener("myDiv", 'click', handleClick);

If specified, the target for the event. This
is likely to be used when relaying a DOM event. If not specified, {@link #method!getTarget #getTarget}
is used to determine the target.

 Returns true if the target of this event is a child of el.  Unless the allowEl parameter is set, it will return false if if the target is el.
 Example usage:<pre><code>
// Handle click on any child of an element
Ext.getBody().on('click', function(e){
    if(e.within('some-el')){
        alert('Clicked on a child of some-el!');
    }
});

// Handle click directly on an element, ignoring clicks on child nodes
Ext.getBody().on('click', function(e,t){
    if((t.id == 'some-el') && !e.within(t, true)){
        alert('Clicked directly on some-el!');
    }
});
</code></pre>

